1
00:00:00,042 --> 00:00:03,008
AI agents need to pay for data and tools.

2
00:00:03,358 --> 00:00:05,271
But with a separate wallet for every agent,

3
00:00:05,292 --> 00:00:07,671
who keeps the whole team on budget?

4
00:00:07,917 --> 00:00:10,733
I built Orbit to give them one shared treasury on NEAR,

5
00:00:10,967 --> 00:00:13,108
with spending limits enforced by the contract.

6
00:00:13,333 --> 00:00:14,000
Let me show you.

7
00:00:14,483 --> 00:00:17,000
Here are our available funds and today’s allowance.

8
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:19,762
I’ll scroll down to the agents sharing that budget.

9
00:00:20,667 --> 00:00:21,983
I’ll open research-scout.

10
00:00:22,333 --> 00:00:23,983
Its key can only request payments,

11
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:26,079
with limits for each payment, the agent,

12
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:27,638
and the shared workspace.

13
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,167
Let’s open this paid account check.

14
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:34,946
Research-scout paid one cent in test tokens to check which keys

15
00:00:34,967 --> 00:00:36,750
could control near dot testnet.

16
00:00:37,033 --> 00:00:39,217
The report found four keys with full control,

17
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:41,633
like administrator credentials for that account.

18
00:00:41,917 --> 00:00:44,095
Its owner should confirm who holds them.

19
00:00:45,417 --> 00:00:47,550
Now let’s look at a separate recorded request.

20
00:00:48,033 --> 00:00:49,850
It hit the operations workspace cap.

21
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,550
Open it, and we can see why it was rejected.

22
00:00:53,017 --> 00:00:54,129
No tokens went out.

23
00:00:55,417 --> 00:00:58,567
We built Orbit, a shared treasury for AI agent teams.

24
00:00:58,867 --> 00:01:01,529
Agents can buy the tools they need while spending stays within

25
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:02,698
the team’s limits.
